  "_comment": "English \"at\" + these nouns is Interslavic v + locative, not na. The prepositions page gives \"na (+ loc.) - on, at\", so na stays the default; the grammar page settles the rest: Interslavic \"is entirely based on forms common to the Slavic languages, and when no common forms exist, on majority solutions\" (http://steen.free.fr/interslavic/grammar.html). Each noun is listed only where the Slavic languages agree on 'in' for it - the forms below, East (ru), West (pl, cs) and South (hr, bg) - and the corpus does not contradict it. Nouns where they split are left out: 'university' is v in ru/bg but na in pl/cs/hr.",
